<p>It usually starts at 3 a.m. A whine at the bedroom door, a frantic dash to the back yard, and then the realization that whatever came out of your dog was not the usual shape. By morning there may have been three more trips, a spot on the rug, and a dog who either looks perfectly cheerful or very sorry for himself.</p>

<p>That last detail matters more than almost anything else. Diarrhea in dogs is extremely common, and most episodes are the gut's way of getting rid of something it didn't like. But the same loose stool can also be the first sign of an infection, a blockage, or a toxin, and the clues that separate the two are usually in how your dog is behaving and what else is going on, not in the stool alone.</p>

<h2>Start by Looking at the Dog, Not the Poop</h2>

<p>Before you think about chicken and rice, take stock. Is your dog still wagging, interested in food, drinking water, and acting like himself between bathroom trips? Or is he flat, hiding, uninterested in dinner, or throwing up too?</p>

<p>PetMD puts the dividing line plainly: "If your dog has diarrhea once and seems otherwise happy and healthy, you can likely watch their condition at home." Cornell University's Riney Canine Health Center starts from the same reassuring place, quoting Dr. Meredith Miller that "many cases of diarrhea in dogs and cats are mild and self-limiting," and then immediately lists the exceptions: a pet that stops eating, is lethargic, has black or tarry stool, is also vomiting, or whose diarrhea "doesn't resolve in 48-72 hours."</p>

<p>So a dog who has loose stool but is bouncing around, eating, and drinking is, most of the time, a wait-and-watch case. A dog who has diarrhea and seems unwell in himself is a phone call.</p>

<h2>Why Dogs Get Diarrhea in the First Place</h2>

<p>VCA Animal Hospitals describes diarrhea as "not a disease, but rather a sign of many different diseases," and that framing is useful. You're not treating diarrhea so much as figuring out what caused it. The usual suspects, which VCA, the AKC and PetMD all list in some form, fall into a few groups.</p>

<p><strong>Something they ate.</strong> This is by far the most common. Vets call it dietary indiscretion, which is a polite way of saying your dog raided the trash, found something dead on a walk, or got rich leftovers from a well-meaning relative. Actually toxic foods are a different problem entirely; if there's any chance your dog ate chocolate, grapes, xylitol or a similar item, check our <a href="/tools/dog-toxic-foods-guide/">dog toxic foods guide</a> and call your vet or a poison line rather than treating it as an ordinary upset stomach.</p>

<p><strong>A sudden food change.</strong> Switching brands overnight, or opening a new bag of a different formula, is a classic cause. Cornell's advice here is specific and easy to follow: go back to the original food until the stool firms up, then restart the switch much more slowly, spreading it "over a week or two" instead of three days.</p>

<p><strong>Stress.</strong> Boarding, travel, a house move, a new baby or a new pet can all show up as loose stool. VCA specifically names stress "following travel, boarding, or other changes in the environment." Stress diarrhea often settles once life gets back to normal.</p>

<p><strong>Parasites and infections.</strong> Worms (hookworms, roundworms, whipworms), giardia and coccidia are all common, especially in puppies and dogs who drink from puddles or spend time where other dogs go to the bathroom. Viral and bacterial infections belong here too, and parvovirus is the one vets worry about most; VCA notes that diarrhea "is one of the first signs seen in parvovirus," which is why an unvaccinated puppy with diarrhea is never a wait-and-see case.</p>

<p>Less often, diarrhea points to something ongoing: food allergies, inflammatory bowel disease, pancreatitis, a swallowed object, side effects from a medication, or disease in the liver, kidneys or other organs. Those causes usually come with other signs, or with diarrhea that keeps coming back, and they need a vet to sort out.</p>

<h2>The Bland-Diet Approach, for Mild Cases</h2>

<p>If your dog is an otherwise healthy adult, acting normally, and has nothing on the warning list below, a short break for the gut followed by a simple, easy-to-digest diet is the standard first step. The AKC quotes Dr. Carly Fox of the Schwarzman Animal Medical Center saying that "in many cases diarrhea can be self-limiting or will respond to a bland diet."</p>

<h3>A short pause on food</h3>

<p>Many vets suggest holding back food for a short window to let the intestines settle. How long varies by source: PetMD suggests six to 12 hours for a dog who is acting fine, while Cornell mentions starting with 12 to 24 hours before introducing a bland diet. That spread is exactly why it's worth a quick call to your own vet, who knows your dog's age, size and history. VCA frames this specifically for "otherwise healthy adult dogs." Puppies, seniors, small breeds and dogs with health conditions like diabetes are a different matter, and PetMD lists puppies, seniors and dogs with pre-existing conditions among the cases that need veterinary care rather than home management.</p>

<p>Water is the one thing you don't hold back. VCA is clear that "a dog with diarrhea can quickly become dehydrated," so keep fresh water available the whole time.</p>

<h3>What to feed a dog with diarrhea</h3>

<p>When it's time to eat again, the classic home remedy is the one most people have heard of. Cornell quotes Dr. Miller: "Mild cases of diarrhea in both cats and dogs can be treated at home by feeding a bland diet such as boiled chicken or low-fat hamburger, and white rice." In practice that means:</p>

<ul>
<li>Lean protein, cooked plain: boiled skinless chicken breast or lean ground beef with the fat drained off.</li>
<li>A simple starch: plain white rice is the usual choice. PetMD also lists potato and pasta as options.</li>
<li>No butter, oil, salt, onion, garlic, seasoning or broth with added salt. "Bland" means genuinely bland.</li>
<li>Small portions offered more often, rather than one big bowl.</li>
</ul>

<p>Plain canned pumpkin (not pie filling) comes up a lot as an add-in for fiber, and PetMD mentions it, but keep it to a small amount and skip it if it seems to make things worse. Ask your vet how much to feed overall, since the right portion depends on your dog's size, and PetMD's own bland-diet guide recommends talking to your veterinarian about portion sizes.</p>

<p>There are also commercial options. Prescription gastrointestinal diets from your vet are made to be easy to digest and, unlike chicken and rice, nutritionally complete. Cornell notes that homemade bland diets "are not appropriate for long-term feeding as they are not nutritionally balanced," so they are meant for a few days, not a new lifestyle.</p>

<h3>Going back to normal food</h3>

<p>Once the stool is firming up, mix the regular food back in gradually. VCA suggests transitioning back over seven to ten days. Skip treats and table scraps until things are fully back to normal; as Dr. Miller puts it, "Owners should avoid excessive treats or rich food until the diarrhea resolves."</p>

<p>One thing to leave out of the home plan: human anti-diarrhea medicine. Cornell specifically warns against giving Pepto-Bismol or Imodium "unless directed by a veterinarian, as they can be harmful to certain dogs," and the AKC's Dr. Fox says most human medications are ineffective and some can be harmful. The same goes for probiotics and supplements; they can help, but VCA recommends checking with your vet first, since product quality varies.</p>

<h2>When Diarrhea Stops Being a Home Problem</h2>

<p>Not every item below means a 2 a.m. emergency visit, but each one means you shouldn't just keep waiting.</p>

<h3>Blood in the diarrhea</h3>

<p>Bright red streaks and dark, tarry stool are two different things. Red blood usually comes from the lower intestine or colon, while black, tar-like stool (called melena) suggests digested blood from higher up, according to the AKC's guide to bloody stool. Cornell lists black or tarry stool, or stool with fresh blood, among the reasons to see a vet.</p>

<p>The AKC's advice is direct: any time you notice blood in your dog's stool, "it's crucial to consult your veterinarian right away," and especially so if it comes with vomiting, weakness or a poor appetite. A few streaks in otherwise normal stool in a dog who's eating happily is less alarming than a large volume of jelly-like bloody diarrhea, but neither one is something to diagnose at home. If your dog is unvaccinated or might have been exposed to parvo, call ahead before you go in, so the clinic can keep other dogs safe.</p>

<h3>Vomiting and diarrhea together</h3>

<p>A dog throwing up and having diarrhea at the same time is losing fluid from both ends, and that combination dehydrates a dog faster than either symptom alone. Cornell points out that vomiting "in addition to diarrhea, further accelerates dehydration," and the AKC's Dr. Fox recommends an emergency visit when diarrhea "occurs with vomiting, lack of appetite, or significant lethargy."</p>

<p>Even if your dog has vomiting and diarrhea but is acting normal, it's worth a call to your vet the same day rather than a few days of waiting. If the vomit itself has an odd color or contains blood, our companion post on <a href="/blog/dog-vomiting-yellow-white-foam-blood/">why dogs throw up yellow or white foam</a> walks through what the different colors usually mean.</p>

<h3>The other red flags</h3>

<p>Pulling together the VCA, PetMD, AKC and Cornell guidance, call your vet promptly if any of these apply:</p>

<ul>
<li>Your dog is lethargic, weak, refusing food, or seems painful in the belly.</li>
<li>There are signs of dehydration, such as sticky gums or unusual tiredness.</li>
<li>Your dog is a puppy, a senior, or has an existing health condition.</li>
<li>You know or suspect they ate something toxic or swallowed an object.</li>
<li>The diarrhea hasn't improved after a day or two of the bland diet, or keeps coming back.</li>
</ul>

<p>If poisoning is a possibility, don't wait for more symptoms. Our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> covers what to do and who to call.</p>

<h2>What About Yellow Diarrhea?</h2>

<p>On its own, yellow or mustard-colored loose stool doesn't tell you much. Stool that rushes through the gut often comes out lighter than usual, which a diet change or mild irritation can cause. Color is one clue your vet uses, alongside consistency, smell and frequency, all of which VCA calls "important in determining the cause." Treat it like any other diarrhea: watch the dog, try the bland diet if he's otherwise fine, and call if it lasts beyond a couple of days or comes with the red flags above.</p>

<h2>A Quick Word on Cleaning It Up</h2>

<p>Carpet is the worst-case scenario. Pick up as much as you can first with paper towels or a plastic bag, lifting rather than wiping so you don't push it deeper into the fibers. Blot (never scrub) with cold water, then use an enzyme cleaner made for pet messes, following the label and letting it sit long enough to break down the residue and odor. Wear gloves and wash your hands afterward, since some of the parasites that cause diarrhea, like giardia, can spread through feces. And if you're heading to the vet, a fresh sample in a clean bag or container is genuinely useful; Cornell suggests bringing one so the vet can see what the diarrhea looks like.</p>

<h2>A Few Common Questions</h2>

<h3>My dog has diarrhea but is acting completely normal. Should I worry?</h3>
<p>Usually not right away. PetMD's guidance is that a dog who otherwise acts healthy and happy can be watched at home, with a short pause on food and then small amounts of bland food. If it doesn't clear up within a day or two, or your dog starts vomiting or acting lethargic, it's time to call.</p>

<h3>How long is too long for dog diarrhea?</h3>
<p>Sources vary a little but cluster around the same window. PetMD says to seek care if it doesn't clear up within 24 to 48 hours, VCA says to consult your vet if frequent loose stools persist for more than two days, and Cornell gives 48 to 72 hours. Puppies and older dogs warrant a shorter fuse.</p>

<h3>Is chicken and rice really the best thing to feed?</h3>
<p>It's a reasonable short-term option for mild cases, and it's what Cornell, the AKC and PetMD all mention. A prescription gastrointestinal diet from your vet does the same job while also being nutritionally complete, which matters if recovery takes more than a few days.</p>

<h3>Can I give my dog Pepto-Bismol or Imodium?</h3>
<p>Not without your vet's say-so. Cornell warns these can be harmful to certain dogs and may interact with other medications. If you think your dog needs medication, call your vet; they can tell you what's safe for your dog specifically.</p>

